<!--
    SANCTIONED EXCEPTION — maintainer-approved, narrowly scoped. This is the ONLY exclusion
    granted anywhere in this repository under the no-suppression rule; the rule's own carve-out
    is for cases where the project's spec mandates an API the analyzer is wrong about (see
    CLAUDE.md, Code Quality). Do not add another <Match> to this file, and do not copy this
    pattern elsewhere, without the maintainer's explicit sign-off.

    (a) Why this is a false positive, not a real finding:

        RFC 3394 AES Key Wrap (the "AES/KW/NoPadding" JCA transform used by
        JceDataKeyProvider.wrapCipher()) DOES provide data integrity: it appends a 64-bit
        Integrity Check Value (the fixed A6A6A6A6A6A6A6A6 initial value carried through the wrap
        transform, RFC 3394 §2.2.3) to every wrapped blob, and unwrap fails — throwing, which
        JceDataKeyProvider converts to DecryptionException.cryptographic(e) — if that ICV does
        not verify. Specs 005 ("JceDataKeyProvider") and 006 §1.2 ("Wrap-scheme tag in
        JceDataKeyProvider") both mandate AES-KW (RFC 3394) as the wrap scheme for this
        provider; this is not a choice this module is free to change to satisfy an analyzer.
        AesKeyWrapKnownAnswerTest exercises the wrap/unwrap round trip against the RFC 3394 KAT
        vectors, and JceDataKeyProviderTest.unwrap_of_a_tampered_blob_is_a_decryption_exception_
        with_the_uniform_message exercises the ICV-tamper-detection path directly (a corrupted
        wrapped blob is rejected as a DecryptionException, not silently accepted).

    (b) Why find-sec-bugs flags it anyway:

        Decompiled com.h3xstream.findsecbugs.crypto.CipherWithNoIntegrityDetector from the
        resolved findsecbugs-plugin 1.14.0 jar. It reports CIPHER_INTEGRITY for any constant
        Cipher.getInstance(...) transform string unless that string matches one of exactly three
        hard-coded escape hatches:

            AUTHENTICATED_CIPHER_MODES = Pattern.compile(".*/(CCM|CWC|OCB|EAX|GCM(-SIV)?)/.*")
            transform.startsWith("RSA")
            transform.equals("ECIES")

        "AES/KW/NoPadding" (the JDK's standard JCA name for AES Key Wrap; "AESWrapPad" has the identical
        problem) matches none of the three: the regex requires a slash-delimited AEAD mode
        segment that key-wrap transforms don't have. The detector's known-safe list covers AEAD
        bulk-cipher modes and RSA/ECIES only — it has no entry for key-wrap constructions, which
        are authenticated by a different mechanism (the ICV) than the modes it recognizes. No
        restructuring of the calling code changes which constant string reaches
        Cipher.getInstance, so no structural fix is possible; see task-6-report.md for the full
        analysis.

    (c) Scope: this is the ONLY sanctioned exclusion in this repository. Any further addition to
        this file, or to any other spotbugs-exclude.xml added to another module, requires the
        maintainer's explicit approval — it is not a precedent for excluding other findings.

    (d) Upstream: report the gap to https://github.com/find-sec-bugs/find-sec-bugs — the
        maintainer will file this; this file is not a substitute for that report.
-->
<FindBugsFilter>
    <Match>
        <Class name="org.jwcarman.codec.crypto.JceDataKeyProvider"/>
        <Method name="wrapCipher"/>
        <Bug pattern="CIPHER_INTEGRITY"/>
    </Match>
</FindBugsFilter>
